.TH BBQ\-PREDICTOR\-SERVE 8 2026\-10\-22 "bbq\-predictor" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
bbq\-predictor\-serve \- serve the published bbq\-predictor archive
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bbq\-predictor\-serve
.B \-\-dir
.I DIR
.RB [ \-\-port
.IR N ]...
.RB [ \-\-user
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ \-\-max\-connections
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-per\-address
.IR N ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Answers bbq\-predictor clients from the signed files that
.B bbq\-predictor \-\-fetch\-once \-\-publish
writes under
.IR DIR .
A request is 48 bytes naming a file; the reply is that file, prebuilt
and held in memory. There is no HTTP and no TLS: the files are signed
with Ed25519 and the applet checks every one, so the transport is not
what is trusted, and this program never holds the signing key.
.PP
Nothing a client sends becomes a path. The tree is loaded into memory at
start, again when the publisher replaces its
.I stations
manifest, and at once on SIGHUP.
.PP
Started as root it binds its ports, then chroots into
.I DIR
and becomes
.I NAME
before reading anything from anybody; it refuses to serve as root at all.
It stays in the foreground and logs to standard error, leaving
backgrounding and log files to the init system.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-dir " DIR"
The published tree.
.TP
.BI \-\-port " N"
A port to listen on, IPv6 and IPv4 alike; up to four. Defaults to 7227
and 443.
.TP
.BI \-\-user " NAME"
The account to serve as. Required when started as root, refused
otherwise.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-connections " N"
Connections held at once; 512 by default, and fewer if the descriptor
limit allows fewer.
.TP
.BI \-\-per\-address " N"
Connections held at once from one address; 8 by default. A client past
either limit is answered busy and closed.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a summary and exit.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Reload the tree now.
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
Close every connection and exit.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/default/bbq\-predictor\-serve
The arguments the init script passes.
.TP
.I /var/lib/bbq\-predictor/published
The tree the packaged service serves.
.TP
.I /var/log/bbq\-predictor\-serve.log
Where the init script sends the log: a line at start and stop, and one
whenever what is served changes.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bbq\-predictor (1)
